# Microeconomics of intertemporal choice in zero-space during Covid-19: a behavioral economics perspective

**Authors:** Javier Cifuentes-Faura, Renaud Di Francesco

## Abstract
Behavioral Economics has been seen to gain importance through its active contribution to reducing, mitigating and hopefully stopping 1 day the propagation of the Covid-19 pandemic. Vaccination and other prevention programs, many qualifying as Non-Pharmaceutical Interventions are supported by the methodology established in Behavioral Economics, to nudge and reinforce actions with positive impact, and deter actions with negative impact. All biases can significantly influence human behavior in a person’s dayto-day life, so it is important to study the meaning of zerospace in a lockdown phase of the Covid-19 pandemic and the activities of household members as agents and beneficiaries. This editorial reviews different aspects of the impact of the pandemic on the economy, and the operational coming into play of behavioral economics tools. It has been shown the nanoeconomics of households under lockdown, modeled using agent models and behavioral economics, where time and activities allocations in time were critical to the continuation of life in the circumstances. From global to local aspects, and bottom–up behavioral incentives

